---
/**
 * ArticleCard (components.md §4) — two variants of the same object.
 *
 *   row  — archive listings, artist pages, "다른 글": dense scanning entries,
 *          a 64px cover on the left of the text block (2026-09-07).
 *   card — the home's browsing grids: cover on top, text below. One shape,
 *          one size, however many cards the section holds, and a step down
 *          from the row variant's type scale — the grids sit below the
 *          Charts grid and must not compete with it.
 *
 * THE `feature` LAYOUT IS GONE (2026-09-07). A section holding exactly one
 * item used to render it as a full-width two-column card, on the argument
 * that a lone card in a four-column grid reads as a defect. The editor chose
 * the opposite trade — empty cells are acceptable, a card changing size
 * between page loads is not — so the home's grids became count-independent
 * and this branch became unreachable. It was deleted rather than left
 * dormant: a layout nobody can reach is a question the next reader has to
 * answer before they can safely change anything here. ChartCard keeps its
 * own `feature`; Charts is a carousel and still switches on count.
 *
 * SIZE IS THE SURFACE'S JOB (2026-09-07). The home shows covers at grid
 * width because the home is for looking; the archive shows them at 64px
 * because the archive is for scanning a list. Same component, same data, two
 * densities — the difference is which variant the page asks for, never a
 * per-page override of the card's internals.
 *
 * D2-R2 (2026-09-07): `showScore` prints the review's figure. It is OPT-IN
 * PER CALL SITE and the default is off, which is what replaces the old
 * type-level guarantee (ArticleItem used to have no score field at all — see
 * lib/derive/lists.ts for the full accounting). Two pages pass it today:
 * the home's 최신 리뷰 section and /archive/reviews/. Every other surface
 * that renders this component — artist pages, the year/genre/tag archives,
 * a review's "다른 글" — deliberately does not, so adding a third is a
 * decision someone makes in a diff.
 *
 * The figure is the ONLY text node of its element, so it stays locatable as
 * `>8.4<` (the dist matrix greps for exactly that, contract §6-4).
 *
 * WHERE THE FIGURE SITS — AND A DIRECTION AMENDMENT (2026-09-07). On the
 * CARD variant it still sits in a plate BUTTED UNDER the cover at zero gap,
 * never on the artwork. On the ROW variant it now sits ON the thumbnail's
 * bottom-right corner, which reverses direction §4.5 ("no crops, no
 * overlays") for that one surface by explicit editorial decision. The rule
 * was cited twice to argue for the adjacent plate and was overruled the
 * third time, so it is amended rather than quietly broken:
 *
 *   §4.5 as it now stands — album art is not this publication's work. It is
 *   never cropped, and it is never overlaid EXCEPT by the list thumbnail's
 *   score chip: a corner mark of about 11% of a 64px thumbnail, opaque so
 *   the figure does not depend on the artwork, and nowhere else on the site.
 *
 * The home's chart card keeps its full-width plate under the cover, and the
 * review hero keeps ScoreMark under the cover. The amendment is scoped to
 * the size where a full-width plate under a 64px square would be a second
 * 64px object — i.e. where the plate would cost more of the row than the
 * chip costs of the art.
 *
 * THE TYPE OVERLINE IS OPT-OUT (`showFormat`, 2026-09-07). It defaults ON,
 * and a surface turns it off when ITS OWN HEADING ALREADY SAYS THE TYPE and
 * nothing of another type can appear under that heading. Both conditions are
 * required, which is why this is a per-surface flag and not a rule the
 * component could apply itself — the card knows what it is, but only the page
 * knows what it is sitting next to. Turned off on four surfaces: the home's
 * Latest Reviews and Notes sections, /archive/reviews/ and /archive/stories/.
 * Left on everywhere the label is INFORMATION rather than an echo — an
 * artist page mixes reviews and stories under one heading, and the archive
 * hub's own list (mixed types, filterable by any axis) is headed by nothing
 * format-specific at all. (Pre-2026-09-09 this list also named the retired
 * per-value `/archive/{year,genre,tag}/` pages, which shared the same
 * reasoning; the axis-chip redesign folded them into the one hub below.)
 *
 * A HOVERED ENTRY TURNS ITS TITLE LAVENDER, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE ANSWER
 * (2026-09-07, both variants). A second state used to sit on top of it — a
 * mint band travelling across the glyphs under the pointer — and it was
 * withdrawn; the style block records what that removed and the blocking
 * accessibility defect it closed on the way out. The colour step itself is
 * not optional decoration: a title that does nothing under the pointer stops
 * reading as a link, and in the row variant the underline that has always
 * been there rides along, so the affordance is never colour alone.
 *
 * The `card` variant carries an EXCERPT (2026-09-07). Title + artist alone
 * made the grid a directory: two cards about wildly different records looked
 * identical apart from the art, and none of the writing — which is the
 * product — was visible before the click. The excerpt is derived, capped and
 * score-screened upstream (deriveLatestArticles); this component only
 * renders it when it is there, and a card with no excerpt is a valid card
 * shape rather than a gap to fill.
 *
 * A story's `subtitle` IS its first paragraph, so the card variant prints
 * the subtitle for reviews only (where it is the artist label) — otherwise a
 * story card would show the same sentence twice at two sizes. The row
 * variant is untouched and still uses subtitle for both types.
 *
 * A story has no album art and never will, so the card variant gives it a
 * typographic block instead of a stock image or a gradient — fabricating
 * imagery to keep a grid tidy is exactly what the no-fabrication rule
 * forbids.
 *
 * At ROW size the same problem has a different answer: a headline set inside
 * a 64px square is not a page of type, it is unreadable. The row gives a
 * story the title's first letter on --surface-3 — the identical geometry
 * CoverImage uses for an album with no cover, because it means the identical
 * thing (there is no artwork, and what is absent should be quiet). It is
 * aria-hidden: the title itself is the next node.
 */
import type { ArticleItem } from '../lib/derive/lists.ts';
import CoverImage from './CoverImage.astro';
import FormatLabel from './FormatLabel.astro';
import { withBase } from '../lib/paths.ts';

interface Props {
  item: ArticleItem;
  variant?: 'row' | 'card';
  /** First grid row loads its covers eagerly; everything below is lazy. */
  eager?: boolean;
  /** Print the review's score (D2-R2) — opt-in per surface, see the intro. */
  showScore?: boolean;
  /** Print the type overline — see the intro. Default on; off only where
   * the surface's own heading already says the type. */
  showFormat?: boolean;
  /** /archive/'s search hub only: the row's precomputed lowercase match text
   * (lib/derive/archive.ts#deriveHubIndex), written as `data-s` on the root
   * `<li>` for enhance.js to read. Every other call site omits it, so the
   * attribute is simply absent there — this component stays ignorant of what
   * a "match" means, it just carries the string. */
  searchKey?: string;
}

const { item, variant = 'row', eager = false, showScore = false, showFormat = true, searchKey } = Astro.props;

// Rendered box: at most 320px from 641px up (the track cap — see
// index.astro's ladder; 4 → 3 → 2 columns all cap at 320px, so one number
// covers every multi-column step). Below 641px the grid is now TWO columns,
// not one (2026-09-08), so the box is a HALF track, not the viewport:
// `calc(50vw - 28px)` is exact, not an approximation — the browsing grid's
// gutter is pinned at its 20px floor for every viewport under ~673px, and
// its gap is a fixed var(--s-16) = 16px, so the arithmetic
// (100vw − 2×20 − 16) ÷ 2 = 50vw − 28 never drifts from the real track
// width the way a rounded guess would. Getting this wrong is not a layout
// bug — it silently downloads the 640 source for a 152px box, roughly 4x
// the bytes the box can ever show.
const FILL_SIZES = '(min-width: 641px) 320px, calc(50vw - 28px)';

// Row thumbnails: 64px displayed, which CoverImage maps to the w320 source
// (w96 is short of 2x at this size). Stories fall through to the letter mark
// below — they have no cover to map.
const ROW_COVER = 64;
// Array.from = code-point safe (astral chars); matches CoverImage's rule.
const rowLetter = Array.from(item.title.trim())[0] ?? '';

// Stories have no score and never will, so the plate simply does not exist
// on a story card — no zero, no dash, no reserved gap.
const score = showScore ? item.score : undefined;
---

{
  variant === 'card' ? (
    <li class="article-card card" data-s={searchKey}>
      <a href={withBase(item.url)} class="card-link">
        <span class="visual">
          {item.type === 'review' ? (
            <CoverImage
              cover={item.cover ?? null}
              size="fill"
              albumTitle={item.title}
              loading={eager ? 'eager' : 'lazy'}
              fillSizes={FILL_SIZES}
            />
          ) : (
            /* aria-hidden: the same words are the card title one line below,
               and hearing the headline twice helps nobody. */
            <span class="type-block" aria-hidden="true">
              <span class="type-block-text">{item.title}</span>
            </span>
          )}
          {score && (
            /* Neutral fill, not the accent plate the chart leader gets: one
               page may carry one large accent surface (§2.3), and six mint
               plates in a browsing grid would turn "the accent means the
               score made this" into wallpaper. */
            <span class="card-score">
              <span class="figure tnum" aria-hidden="true">{score}</span>
              {/* A bare numeral is meaningless read aloud. */}
              <span class="sr-only">평점 {score} / 10점 만점</span>
            </span>
          )}
        </span>
        <span class="body">
          {showFormat && <FormatLabel text={item.formatLabel} variant="chip" />}
          <span class="title">{item.title}</span>
          {item.type === 'review' && item.subtitle && <span class="subtitle">{item.subtitle}</span>}
          {item.excerpt && <span class="excerpt">{item.excerpt}</span>}
          <time class="date tnum" datetime={item.date}>
            {item.date}
          </time>
        </span>
      </a>
    </li>
  ) : (
    <li class="article-card" data-s={searchKey}>
      <a href={withBase(item.url)} class="row-card-link">
        <span class="thumb">
          {item.type === 'review' ? (
            <CoverImage
              cover={item.cover ?? null}
              size={ROW_COVER}
              albumTitle={item.title}
              loading={eager ? 'eager' : 'lazy'}
            />
          ) : (
            /* aria-hidden: the title is the very next node — see the intro. */
            <span class="type-mark" aria-hidden="true">{rowLetter}</span>
          )}
          {score && (
            /* The figure sits ON the thumbnail's bottom-right corner — see
               the intro for the §4.5 amendment this is. */
            <span class="thumb-score">
              <span class="figure tnum" aria-hidden="true">{score}</span>
              <span class="sr-only">평점 {score} / 10점 만점</span>
            </span>
          )}
        </span>
        <span class="text">
          {showFormat && <span class="format">{item.formatLabel}</span>}
          <span class="title">{item.title}</span>
          {item.subtitle && <span class="subtitle">{item.subtitle}</span>}
        </span>
        <time class="date tnum" datetime={item.date}>
          {item.date}
        </time>
      </a>
    </li>
  )
}

<style>
  /* ── row ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  /* Thumbnail column + the original two-column text block beside it. The
     thumb spans all three text rows, so the row height is max(64px, text) —
     which on a two-line entry is the text, i.e. the archive did not get
     taller for most rows. */
  /* THREE COLUMNS, ONE BASELINE OF ALIGNMENT (rebuilt 2026-09-07). The row
     used to be a 3x3 area grid whose top row held the type overline on the
     left and the date on the right. Two of this round's changes emptied that
     row on the listing pages — the overline came off where the page heading
     already says the type, and the figure moved onto the thumbnail — and a
     reserved-but-empty row left the title floating high against a 64px
     thumbnail. Rather than patch the gap, the row is now what it always
     described: a thumbnail, a stack of text, and a date, vertically centred
     against each other. The overline, where a mixed listing still needs it,
     is the first line of the text stack instead of a separate row. */
  .row-card-link {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: auto minmax(0, 1fr) auto;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--s-16);
    padding: var(--s-12) var(--s-8);
    min-height: 44px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }
  .thumb {
    display: block;
    flex: none;
    /* Positioning context for the score chip below — the chip is a SIBLING
       of the picture, not a child, so the cover frame's overflow:hidden (and
       the hover zoom that lives inside it) cannot clip or scale it. */
    position: relative;
  }
  /* The text stack: optional overline, title, subtitle. min-width:0 lets the
     nowrap subtitle ellipsise instead of widening the column. */
  .text {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--s-4);
    min-width: 0;
  }
  /* ── The score, ON the cover (2026-09-07) ──
     Deliberately the smallest mark that stays readable: ~26x18 on a 64px
     thumbnail is about 11% of the artwork, in the corner, and it never
     crosses the image. See the component intro for the direction §4.5
     amendment.
     OPAQUE, not a translucent wash: a cover can be any colour, and the one
     thing that must not depend on the artwork is whether the figure can be
     read. --surface-3 on --ink is 12.48:1 over any cover that will ever
     exist, because the cover is not in the calculation. The pair is the same
     one .card-score uses, so the two score marks are one object at two
     sizes rather than two inventions. */
  .thumb-score {
    position: absolute;
    right: 0;
    bottom: 0;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    min-width: 26px;
    height: 18px;
    padding: 0 var(--s-4);
    background: var(--surface-3);
    color: var(--ink);
    /* Follows the cover's own corner so the chip reads as part of the same
       stamped object rather than a sticker on top of it. */
    border-bottom-right-radius: var(--r-2);
  }
  .thumb-score .figure {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: var(--fs-12);
    /* line-height 1 + a centred flex box: the figure's own line box carries
       no leading to push the digits off the chip's centre (see the plate
       note in ScoreMark). */
    line-height: 1;
    font-weight: 800;
  }
  /* A story has no artwork: same square, same weight, the title's first
     letter — identical to CoverImage's missing-cover state, because it means
     the same thing. */
  .type-mark {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: start;
    width: 64px;
    height: 64px;
    background: var(--surface-3);
    color: var(--muted);
    font-family: var(--display);
    font-weight: 800;
    font-size: calc(64px * 0.42);
    line-height: 1;
    border-radius: var(--r-2);
    user-select: none;
  }
  .format {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: var(--fs-12);
    line-height: 16px;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: var(--label-ls);
    /* Same treatment as FormatLabel — the row overline is the same object. */
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  .row-card-link .title {
    font-size: var(--fs-16);
    line-height: 22px;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--ink);
    transition: color var(--t-micro);
  }
  .row-card-link:hover .title {
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
  }
  .subtitle {
    font-size: var(--fs-14);
    line-height: 20px;
    color: var(--muted);
    overflow: hidden;
    text-overflow: ellipsis;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .date {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: var(--fs-12);
    line-height: 16px;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    font-weight: 500;
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  /* ── card ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .card {
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--r-0);
    transition: background var(--t-micro), border-color var(--t-micro);
  }
  .card:hover {
    background: var(--surface-2);
    border-color: var(--line-strong);
  }
  .card-link {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    height: 100%;
  }
  .visual {
    display: block;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }
  /* Story card: the headline IS the artwork. Bottom-left set, clipped by
     the square — a page of type, not a picture of one. */
  .type-block {
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-end;
    aspect-ratio: 1 / 1;
    padding: var(--s-12);
    background: var(--surface-2);
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .type-block-text {
    font-family: var(--display);
    font-size: var(--fs-27);
    line-height: 30px;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    font-weight: 800;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  /* Zero gap under the art, full width: the cover and the figure read as one
     object while not one pixel of the artwork is covered (§4.5). Same
     grammar as ChartCard's rank plate. */
  /* `align-items: center` and a line-height of 1, not `baseline` and a 20px
     line box. A single-line flex row aligned on the BASELINE is placed at
     the START of the container, so every pixel that min-height adds lands
     under the figure and the number rides high in its own plate. Centring
     the box is half the fix; killing the line box's leading is the other
     half, because a 20px line box around a 15px digit reserves descender
     room that digits never use and biases the ink upward again. The same
     two-part correction is applied to ScoreMark and ChartCard's rank plate —
     the three plates are one object at three sizes. */
  .card-score {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: flex-end;
    align-items: center;
    min-height: 28px;
    padding: var(--s-4) var(--s-8);
    background: var(--surface-3);
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .figure {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: var(--fs-15);
    line-height: 1;
    font-weight: 800;
  }
  /* One notch down the existing scale from the row variant's headline — no
     new sizes. At the 320px track cap a full 21/26 headline wrapped to three
     lines on ordinary album titles and pushed the date out of alignment with
     its neighbours (every card in the browsing grids renders at this width;
     there is no wider caller to size for — see FILL_SIZES above). */
  .body {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: flex-start;
    gap: var(--s-4);
    padding: var(--s-12);
  }
  .card .title {
    font-family: var(--display);
    font-size: var(--fs-17);
    line-height: 24px;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--ink);
    /* Two-line clamp with zero JS; the third line would push the date out of
       alignment with the neighbouring cards. */
    display: -webkit-box;
    -webkit-box-orient: vertical;
    -webkit-line-clamp: 2;
    line-clamp: 2;
    overflow: hidden;
    transition: color var(--t-micro);
  }
  /* ── Hover: the entry answers, in ONE step ───────────────────────────
     Pointer anywhere on the entry → the title turns flat lavender, and in
     the row variant the underline it has always had comes with it. That is
     the whole hover vocabulary of this component, on both variants. Keeping
     a visible response is not optional: an entry whose title does nothing
     under the pointer stops reading as a link, and colour alone would not be
     enough for the row either (WCAG 1.4.1) — hence the underline.

     WHAT WAS REMOVED (2026-09-07), AND THE DEFECT THAT LEFT WITH IT. A
     second state used to sit on top of this one: hovering the TITLE ITSELF
     ran a mint band (--title-spot) across the glyphs under the pointer,
     painted as a gradient clipped to the text with `background-clip: text` +
     `color: transparent`. It was withdrawn by editorial decision, and the
     withdrawal closed a blocking accessibility defect in the same edit.

     The defect is recorded here because its SHAPE will recur. Clipped text
     needs a forced-colors escape hatch, and that rule declared two things at
     once — `color: CanvasText` and `forced-color-adjust: none`. Astro scopes
     component rules with an attribute selector, so the hatch's `.title-spot`
     computed as (0,2,0) while the plain `.card .title` next to it computed
     as (0,4,0):

       · `color: CanvasText`         LOST the specificity fight — the title
                                     kept this palette's dark-page ink;
       · `forced-color-adjust: none` WON, having no competitor at all — the
                                     UA was told not to substitute colours.

     That combination is strictly worse than either half. The element opted
     OUT of the reader's high-contrast palette while holding a colour chosen
     for a dark page: measured under `forced-colors: active`, #E8E9F2 on a
     #FFFFFF system canvas, 1.21:1, on every article list this component
     renders — and on those pages the title is the only thing to click.

     `forced-color-adjust` existed ONLY to protect the gradient, so removing
     the gradient removed the reason for it. There is deliberately no
     forced-colors rule in this component now: the titles are ordinary text
     and take the system ink like every other text node on the page. The
     general form, since this is the second specificity fight lost at this
     spot: a rule that sets forced-color-adjust must be at least as specific
     as every rule that sets `color` on the same element, or it disables the
     reader's last colour control and loses the colour too. */
  .card-link:hover .title,
  .row-card-link:hover .title {
    color: var(--accent-2-ink);
  }
  .card .subtitle {
    max-width: 100%;
    font-size: var(--fs-13);
    line-height: 18px;
  }
  /* Two lines of the piece itself. Clamped rather than trimmed to length so
     the cut lands on a line boundary at whatever width the grid resolves to
     — the derive layer caps the string at 96 characters, which is roughly
     two lines in the widest card and more than two in the narrow ones.
     Still two lines at the 320px track cap: that is ~36 Korean characters, a
     readable fragment of a sentence. Below two lines the excerpt stops being
     prose and becomes a label, and it would be better dropped than kept. */
  .excerpt {
    font-size: var(--fs-13);
    line-height: 18px;
    color: var(--muted);
    display: -webkit-box;
    -webkit-box-orient: vertical;
    -webkit-line-clamp: 2;
    line-clamp: 2;
    overflow: hidden;
  }
</style>
